.TH SAVEMEM 1.SH NAMEsavemem, loadmem \- copy memory to a file and back.SH SYNOPSIS\fBsavemem\fRfrom to path.br\fBloadmem\fRdestination limit path.SH DESCRIPTION\fBsavemem\fRwrites the block of memory from address\fIfrom\fRto address\fIto\fR,\fRinclusive, to a new file:\fBsavemem 8000 8027 blk\fRwrites forty bytes.  The file must not exist already; given one thatdoes it prints its usage instead of overwriting..PP\fBloadmem\fRis the reverse: it copies the file into memory starting at\fIdestination\fR,\fRrefusing to go past\fIlimit\fR,\fRand says nothing when it works.  Read the same range back with\fBsavemem\fRto check..PPAddresses are in hexadecimal.  Both need the super user..SH SEE ALSOdload, dump